@* The line a page holds the table's place with while its store is being read.

   It is the whole of what a reader sees of a slow store, so it says the same thing in the same
   words wherever it stands: a turning ring, which says something is happening, and a sentence,
   which says what. The ring is drawn by CSS rather than as one more SectionMark — the marks are a
   set of still glyphs standing for places, and this is neither still nor a place.

   The sentence is written here rather than handed in by each page, for the reason the tiles are one
   component: six copies of one sentence are six chances for five of them to say it differently.
   Written as markup rather than as a value, too, so the ellipsis reaches the page as an ellipsis —
   a value is encoded on the way out, and "Loading data&#x2026;" is the same three dots to a browser
   and a different string to everything else that reads this page.

   Announced rather than silent: on these pages the reader has just followed a link and the page
   they land on has no rows yet, so the one line saying why is the one line worth reading out. *@
<p class="loading" role="status">
    <span class="spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span>Loading data…</span>
</p>
@* The one reader this line would lie to.

   The page is sent in two parts and the second is put in place by the browser, so with no
   scripting the first part is the whole of it and the line above goes on saying the store is being
   read for as long as the page is open. There is nothing to be done about that from here — it is
   how a page is sent before its store has answered — so the reader is told, in the place they are
   waiting, rather than left waiting. Everything else on these pages still works without a script:
   the narrowing, the order, the pages and the fold are all in the address. *@
<noscript>
    <p class="missing">
        The rows are sent after the page and put in place by the browser, which needs scripting
        turned on. Everything else here is in the address and works without it.
    </p>
</noscript>
